<h2>The hearing in outline</h2>
<p>The petitioner invoked Section 11(6) of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 for appointment of a sole arbitrator in a dispute valued at about Rs 3 crore with a well-known real estate developer. Before Justice Om Prakash Shukla, the respondent&rsquo;s counsel did not dispute the existence of the arbitration clause, and the parties were in agreement that the disputes should be referred. What followed was the modern Section 11 order in its cleanest form.</p>

<h2>The post-Krish Spinning discipline</h2>
<p>The Court restated the governing principle from <em>SBI General Insurance Co. Ltd. v. Krish Spinning</em>, 2024 SCC OnLine SC 1754: the referral court examines only whether an arbitration agreement exists prima facie. The deeper questions that once consumed Section 11 hearings — whether the claims are time-barred, whether a discharge voucher extinguished them, whether the dispute is frivolous — now belong to the tribunal under the competence-competence principle. With the clause undisputed, the Court recorded that there was no impediment to the reference.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Appointment.</strong> Ms. Lalit Mohini Bhat, Advocate, was appointed sole arbitrator.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Institutional anchor.</strong> The arbitration is to proceed under the aegis of the Delhi International Arbitration Centre, with fees per the DIAC schedule.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Timelines and disclosures.</strong> The arbitrator is to enter upon the reference within two weeks and make the Section 12(2) disclosures of independence and impartiality within two weeks of doing so.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Everything else left open.</strong> All pleas of jurisdiction and arbitrability remain open before the tribunal; the Court&rsquo;s observations bind no one on merits.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why routine orders like this matter</h2>
<p>The value of a decision like this lies in its predictability. For commercial parties in Delhi, the sequence is now well-marked: a Section 21 notice invoking the clause; a Section 11 petition if the counterparty fails to cooperate in constituting the tribunal; a hearing confined to the existence of the agreement; and an institutional reference, typically to DIAC, with fee certainty under its schedule. The compressed enquiry has two practical consequences. First, respondents gain little by resisting appointment on merits-flavoured grounds — those arguments are not lost, but their forum is the tribunal, raised through a Section 16 application if jurisdictional. Second, petitioners should ensure the formal foundations are unimpeachable, because they are the only things the court examines: the contract containing the clause, the Section 21 notice and its service, and the petition&rsquo;s compliance with limitation for invoking arbitration.</p>

<h2>Judicial appointment and institutional arbitration</h2>
<p>The routing of court appointments through the Delhi International Arbitration Centre reflects a sustained institutional turn in Delhi practice. DIAC administration brings a published fee schedule — avoiding fee disputes that unsettled ad hoc references — along with case management and prescribed timelines that dovetail with the twelve-month mandate under Section 29A. Parties drafting fresh contracts can capture these benefits directly by naming institutional rules in the clause itself, rather than arriving at them through a court appointment after a dispute has soured cooperation.</p>
<div class="note">Section 11(6) petitions in Delhi lie to the High Court for arbitrations seated in Delhi. The one-year window from the Section 21 notice is a live issue after recent jurisprudence on limitation for Section 11 petitions, so parties should move promptly once the notice period lapses. This article is informational and not advice in any matter.</div>
